base: Use udf instead of bkpt for ImmediateCrash() on ARMv7

Upstream's TRAP_SEQUENCE1_() for 32-bit ARM is bkpt #0. On Raspberry Pi OS
32-bit kernels (verified on 6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v7, Pi 3B), the kernel's
hw_breakpoint layer consumes the BKPT debug-event Prefetch Abort without
delivering any signal, and since the architectural exception return address
for a Prefetch Abort is the aborting instruction itself (ARM ARM DDI 0406C
B1.9.7), the CPU re-executes bkpt #0 forever. A minimal C program executing
bkpt #0 with default signal dispositions spins at 100% CPU indefinitely
with no pending/blocked/caught signals and nothing in dmesg.

As a result, every base::ImmediateCrash() on armhf - including release-mode
CHECK/NOTREACHED failures - hangs the process instead of terminating it.
With chromium 149 (GpuPersistentCache enabled by default) a GPU-process
shader-cache bind failure reaches ImmediateCrash() at startup, leaving the
GPU process spinning at 100% CPU; the browser never detects the crash and
hangs with no window (RPi-Distro/chromium#60).

Use udf #0 instead, which raises SIGILL and terminates reliably on both
32-bit kernels and 64-bit kernels running 32-bit userspace (verified on the
Pi 3B). SIGILL is an ordinary fatal crash signal, so crashpad and the
browser's GPU crash detection keep working. Upstream already uses udf #0 as
TRAP_SEQUENCE2_() and tracks removing bkpt in crbug.com/40625592.
--- a/base/immediate_crash.h
+++ b/base/immediate_crash.h
@@ -76,12 +76,11 @@ extern "C" int __attribute__((weak)) __l
 
 #elif defined(ARCH_CPU_ARMEL)
 
-// bkpt will generate a SIGBUS when running on armv7 and a SIGTRAP when running
-// as a 32 bit userspace app on arm64. There doesn't seem to be any way to
-// cause a SIGTRAP from userspace without using a syscall (which would be a
-// problem for sandboxing).
-// TODO(crbug.com/40625592): Remove bkpt from this sequence.
-#define TRAP_SEQUENCE1_() asm volatile("bkpt #0")
+// Upstream uses bkpt #0 here, but Raspberry Pi OS 32-bit kernels deliver no
+// signal for the BKPT debug event and the exception return re-executes the
+// instruction, so bkpt spins forever instead of crashing. udf raises SIGILL
+// on both 32-bit and 64-bit kernels.
+#define TRAP_SEQUENCE1_() asm volatile("udf #0")
 #define TRAP_SEQUENCE2_() asm volatile("udf #0")
 
 #elif defined(ARCH_CPU_ARM64)
